     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Ashwin Expresses 'Genuine Worry' Over Indian Bowling After Ranchi Loss</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#West Indies</span> <span>#R Ashwin</span></div>
       <p>India's bowling attack faltered in the second T20I at the JSCA International Stadium Complex in Ranchi on October 9, 2026, allowing West Indies to successfully chase down 249 runs. Following the match, off-spinner Ravichandran Ashwin voiced &quot;genuine worry&quot; about the future of Indian bowling and openly questioned tactical decisions made during the game, which saw the five-match series draw level at 1-1.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/ashwin-expresses-genuine-worry-over-indian-bowling-after-ran.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>West Indies secured a convincing victory in 18.3 overs, propelled by an unbeaten 102 from 45 deliveries by Shai Hope and a rapid 84 from 37 balls by Sherfane Rutherford, who together forged a crucial 120-run partnership. India had posted a formidable total of 249 for 5, built on Sanju Samson's 82 off 41 balls and Tilak Varma's 44 off 18 balls, but this proved insufficient. Shreyas Iyer captained India, while Shai Hope led West Indies.</p><p>Speaking on his YouTube channel, Ashwin highlighted what he perceived as a lack of quality in India's bowling resources and insufficient emphasis on developing bowlers equipped for challenging conditions. He stated, &quot;It’s not that I’m blaming the bowlers, but we don’t have enough quality in our bowling, and we haven’t placed enough emphasis on developing it.&quot; Ashwin expressed hope for more dedicated efforts to groom high-quality bowlers, noting that &quot;bowling in such conditions requires understanding and game awareness,&quot; a quality he felt was &quot;lacking a little today.&quot;</p><p>Ashwin drew a comparison to previous Indian captains like Rohit Sharma and Suryakumar Yadav, whom he described as &quot;fortunate&quot; to have Jasprit Bumrah as a &quot;cheat code.&quot; He elaborated that Bumrah's rare ability to bowl defensively when required and deploy a varied range of slower deliveries provided a significant edge, a quality he believes is not commonly found among current bowlers. This unique talent, Ashwin suggested, sets Bumrah apart and underpins his concern for the broader landscape of Indian bowling.</p><p>The veteran spinner also critiqued specific tactical choices during the Ranchi encounter. He felt India &quot;lacked a bit of defensive bowling&quot; and questioned the timing of Shivam Dube's introduction into the attack immediately after the powerplay. Despite Mayank Yadav taking the first two or three wickets within the opening six overs, reducing West Indies to 5 for 2, Ashwin suggested that Kuldeep Yadav should have been brought into the bowling rotation &quot;a little earlier&quot; to capitalize on the early advantage. He concluded that both captains, Shai Hope and Shreyas Iyer, &quot;had very average days today.&quot;</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report is thorough, accurately citing the source's quotes and tactical analysis.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/genuinely-worried-about-the-future-of-indian-bowling-r-ashwin-after-ranchi-loss-2626/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
