     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-10-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by BBC World, Al Jazeera">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>'Cockroach' Leaders Detained as Delhi Faces Lockdown Amid Electoral Reform Protest</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#India</span> <span>#Protest</span> <span>#Electoral Reforms</span> <span>#Detentions</span></div>
       <p>Hundreds of protesters, including leaders of the Gen-Z led Cockroach Janta Party (CJP), have been detained in Delhi during a demonstration demanding electoral reforms. The Indian capital experienced &quot;lockdown-like scenes&quot; (Al Jazeera) with tens of thousands of security personnel deployed and roads barricaded ahead of the planned protest. Delhi Police had refused permission for the demonstration to take place at Jantar Mantar, a landmark in the city.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/cockroach-leaders-detained-as-delhi-faces-lockdown-amid-elec.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>CJP founder Abhijeet Dipke, along with leaders Saurav Das and Ashutosh Ranka, were reportedly detained shortly after landing in Delhi from Mumbai on Saturday. The group claimed the three men were taken to an undisclosed location, though Delhi Police has not commented on the detentions. Student activist Neha Bora was also detained, urging demonstrators to &quot;hold the line.&quot; Before his detention, Dipke stated, &quot;If you think you can stop us by threatening us with jail or trying to intimidate us with the fear of imprisonment, you are mistaken.&quot;</p><p>The CJP is advocating for electoral reforms just months before crucial state elections and has demanded the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner (CEC) Gyanesh Kumar. The group and opposition parties accuse Kumar of manipulating voters' lists and favoring Prime Minister Narendra Modi's Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) through a nationwide exercise to update electoral rolls. While the Election Commission denies these allegations, the exercise has faced controversy in multiple states, with tens of thousands claiming wrongful removal.</p><p>Security measures were extensive, preventing large numbers of expected protesters from reaching Jantar Mantar. Announcements were broadcast across the city, asking for public cooperation. Several trains to and from major Delhi stations were cancelled, and dozens of metro stations were closed from Friday night. Buses packed with detained protesters were observed being driven away from protest sites. One protester, Sabika Muzaffar, told the BBC she expected to be detained, noting she and her mother were &quot;rote learning our lawyer's number&quot; on their way to the protest site; she was taken away by police shortly after.</p><p>The CJP emerged as a significant protest movement in May, stemming from a remark by India's Chief Justice Surya Kant, who had likened some jobless young people to &quot;cockroaches.&quot; Dipke, who previously worked with the opposition Aam Aadmi Party (AAP) and had just completed a master's degree, posted on X, &quot;What if all cockroaches come together?&quot; The post went viral, leading to the CJP's formation. The movement gained momentum, tapping into public anger over unemployment, exam paper leaks, and government accountability, and previously achieved a success in July with the resignation of India's education minister after a five-week protest.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively synthesizes the source material and includes necessary context from the secondary outlets mentioned.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 2 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c65yn7xvde7vo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li><li><a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/10/indias-cockroach-leaders-detained-new-delhi-in-lockdown?traffic_source=rss" rel="noopener">Al Jazeera</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 2 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/delhi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Delhi</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
