     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-10 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Himalayan Flood Risk Rises as Building Accelerates, Study Finds</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Himalayas</span> <span>#Flooding</span> <span>#Climate Change</span></div>
       <p>Nearly 300,000 people may live in areas at risk of flooding across the Nepalese, Indian, and Chinese regions of the Himalayas, according to a study published on October 10, 2026, in Scientific Reports. The research, conducted by C. Scott Watson and colleagues, reveals a significant acceleration in construction within potential flood zones. Between 2016 and 2023, the total area covered by buildings in these vulnerable regions increased by approximately 45.75%.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/himalayan-flood-risk-rises-as-building-accelerates-study-fin.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study assessed changes in the potential exposure of buildings and people to flooding by examining areas up to 50 kilometers (31 miles) downstream of 1,361 Himalayan lakes in Nepal and neighboring parts of India and China. Researchers utilized five data sets, validated against manually recorded data, to estimate the total building footprint. This footprint, specifically for buildings within 50 vertical meters (164 feet) of a river channel—used as a proxy for flood exposure—rose from 12.38 to 18.04 square kilometers (4.78 to 6.97 square miles) over the seven-year period. In areas identified as being at high risk of glacial lake outburst floods (GLOFs), the building footprint saw an even more pronounced increase of 77.78%, expanding from 0.72 to 1.28 square kilometers (0.28 to 0.49 square miles).</p><p>Based on these building footprints, the authors estimated that by 2026, approximately 293,800 people—representing 18% of the population within the study area—may reside in buildings exposed to potential flooding. This figure includes an estimated 108,500 people in Nepal, with 22,800 of them living within 10 vertical meters (33 feet) of a river channel. Furthermore, the population living within these flood risk zones is projected to increase by 17,900 individuals, or 6%, between 2015 and 2030. Flooding in the Himalayan region is typically caused by seasonal monsoons, extreme rainfall, or rare but devastating debris flows or floods from glacial lakes, known as glacial lake outburst floods. Climate change is predicted to increase the severity of these events, while the steep, narrow valleys characteristic of mountainous areas can exacerbate flood heights and their subsequent effects, making risk assessment particularly challenging.</p><p>C. Scott Watson and colleagues caution that their estimates for both total building footprint and population growth carry high uncertainty. They underscore the critical need for more accurate building databases to enable better calculation of risk and to inform mitigation strategies in these rapidly developing, high-mountain catchments. This research was edited by Lisa Lock and reviewed by Robert Egan.</p><p>What to watch: Future efforts to develop more precise building databases and risk assessment models in the Himalayas.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is comprehensive, accurately reflects all provided statistics, and maintains a professional tone consistent with the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-himalayan-zone.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
