     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-10 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>MCC Clarifies New Run-Out Rule Amidst Widespread Misinterpretation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#MCC</span> <span>#Laws of Cricket</span></div>
       <p>The Marylebone Cricket Club (MCC) intervened on October 8, 2026, to dispel widespread confusion and misinterpretation surrounding its updated run-out law, which came into effect on October 1, 2026. The MCC clarified that the 'complete control' requirement, a key point of contention, applies exclusively when a fielder breaks the wicket using their hand or arm while simultaneously holding the ball.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/mcc-clarifies-new-run-out-rule-amidst-widespread-misinterpre.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Crucially, the MCC stated that if the ball directly strikes the stumps – whether deflected by a bowler, ricocheted off a wicketkeeper's pads, or kicked onto the stumps – no 'complete control' requirement is applicable, and the run-out stands. This clarification firmly confirms that non-striker run-outs, often a subject of debate, remain fully valid under the new law, ensuring continuity in established cricketing dismissals.</p><p>The rule change itself was initially announced in February 2026 and forms a significant part of the MCC's 2026 edition of the Laws of Cricket. This comprehensive update includes 73 material revisions, marking it as the first major overhaul of the Laws since 2022. The MCC acknowledged the confusion, stating, as quoted by NDTV Sports, that &quot;Although this Law change has been public since February, when the Laws came into force last week a number of people appear to have misinterpreted it.&quot;</p><p>Confusion rapidly spread after the new laws were implemented in international cricket on October 1, 2026. Fans widely speculated that various scenarios, such as a bowler deflecting the ball onto the stumps or the ball bouncing off a wicketkeeper's pads, would no longer result in run-outs under the revised regulations. The MCC directly addressed these concerns: &quot;There has been a suggestion that 'for all run outs, the fielder has to have complete control of the ball', which has led to questions about bowlers deflecting the ball onto the stumps, or it bouncing off a wicket-keeper's pads, or being kicked onto the stumps - and there's been some disquiet about a new Law saying you need complete control for any Run out.&quot;</p><p>Reaffirming its position, the MCC elaborated: &quot;Fortunately, that's not what the Law says, and that's not what you'll be seeing in cricket going forward in these new Laws and Playing Conditions. This Law applies to when a fielder is breaking the wicket with their hand or arm, while holding the ball. In that circumstance, they need to have the ball in their hand, and with complete control, to be able to break the wicket.&quot; The rationale behind introducing the specific 'complete control' requirement for hand-or-arm run-outs was due to advances in broadcast technology. Improved television coverage had increasingly highlighted instances where batters were dismissed even when the fielder had minimal or no control of the ball at the exact moment of breaking the wicket, which was inconsistent with the Law's long-standing requirement of 'holding the ball'.</p><p>Under the updated law, a fielder must genuinely hold the ball with complete control when actively using their hand or arm to break the wicket. The new provision does not, however, alter the established position for run-outs where the ball strikes the stumps directly without any involvement from a fielder's hand or arm. The MCC's 2026 revision package, encompassing 73 material changes, represents its most comprehensive update to the Laws of Cricket since 2022.</p><p>What to watch: How teams adapt to the clarified rule, particularly in close run-out situations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately covers the MCC's clarification regarding the run-out rule, including the specific reasoning and context provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
