     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-10-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Nobel Laureate Navi Pillay Condemns US Sanctions on ICC</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Nobel Peace Prize</span> <span>#ICC</span> <span>#US Sanctions</span> <span>#Navi Pillay</span></div>
       <p>Navi Pillay, the recipient of the 2026 Nobel Peace Prize, has described US sanctions against the International Criminal Court (ICC) as &quot;unheard of&quot; and &quot;unacceptable.&quot; Pillay, a South African former ICC judge, was awarded the prize on Friday for her efforts to promote peace and international law. Speaking via video link during an event at the Nobel Peace Center in Oslo on Saturday, she highlighted the tension surrounding the continued existence of international courts, specifically the ICC in The Hague. She stated, &quot;Sanctions have been announced by one country against the judges, just for the judicial orders they issue. This is unheard of, unacceptable.&quot;</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/nobel-laureate-navi-pillay-condemns-us-sanctions-on-icc.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The sanctions were unveiled by the US administration hours after President Donald Trump expressed dissatisfaction with the prize selection. US Secretary of State Marco Rubio stated that the measures, which include a ban on US entities making transactions with the ICC, are intended to &quot;defend US sovereignty&quot; and accused the court of seeking to prosecute Americans. The US has already sanctioned 17 ICC judges and officials, but these new measures target the court itself as an institution to inhibit its financial operations. A six-month grace period has been provided before some sanctions take effect, allowing member states time to negotiate a solution.</p><p>The ICC characterized the sanctions as an assault on the rule of law and the foundations of the international legal order. UN Secretary General Antonio Guterres called the decision a &quot;serious blow&quot; to international criminal justice. The UK, along with Canada, Denmark, Germany, France, Italy, Japan, and the Netherlands, stated they &quot;regret and strongly disagree with&quot; the sanctions. Trump, who called the announcement of Pillay as the winner &quot;disgraceful and embarrassing,&quot; has long indicated he believed he deserved the prize. Pillay, 85, previously served as UN rights chief and is South Africa's fifth Nobel Peace Prize winner, following Nelson Mandela and Archbishop Desmond Tutu.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive overview of the situation, including the specific reactions from the ICC, UN, and various nations, as well as the background of the Nobel prize controversy.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqkgjrpmr47vo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
