     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-10 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rohit Sharma Reflects on 2023 World Cup Heartbreak, Fuels Future Triumphs</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Rohit Sharma</span> <span>#World Cup</span> <span>#India Cricket</span></div>
       <p>Former India captain Rohit Sharma has candidly shared his experience of losing the 2023 ODI World Cup final at home, labeling it as the &quot;toughest phase of his cricketing career.&quot; Speaking on his programme *Family Full House with Rohit Sharma*, the veteran batter detailed how this defeat against Australia profoundly impacted the Indian team, instilling a renewed sense of &quot;desperation and hunger&quot; that ultimately propelled them to victory in the 2024 T20 World Cup, as reported by Crictracker.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/rohit-sharma-reflects-on-2023-world-cup-heartbreak-fuels-fut.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>India had entered the 2023 World Cup final at the Narendra Modi Stadium in Ahmedabad on November 19, having secured 10 consecutive victories in the tournament. However, Pat Cummins' Australian side delivered a memorable performance, with Travis Head scoring a match-winning century to clinch the title and end India's unbeaten run. Sharma, who has been playing since 2007, making it 19 years in his career, stated, &quot;Actually, mere saare cricketing career mein sabse tough period tha ye... Aur 19 saal mein maine aisa time kabhi bhi nahi dekha apne khud ke liye.&quot; He contrasted this with a difficult personal period in Sri Lanka in 2012, highlighting that the 2023 loss was a national goal, not just his own.</p><p>The defeat was particularly agonizing for the Indian team, given their dominant performance throughout the tournament on home soil. Sharma underscored this sentiment, explaining, &quot;Aur kharaab isiliye laga kyunki hum India mein World Cup khel rahe the aur hum aise khel rahe the ki kabhi laga nahi tha ki hum haar sakte hain.&quot; He acknowledged that such setbacks are an inherent part of sports, where unexpected disappointments can occur even when &quot;sab kuch sahi chal raha hai.&quot; This realization, he noted, is a crucial lesson learned from sport.</p><p>Despite the profound disappointment, Rohit believes the 2023 setback served as a crucial turning point in India's white-ball cricket journey. He stated that the loss taught the team &quot;bahut kuch&quot; and suggested that &quot;Agar woh hota nahi, toh shayad hum 2024 mein T20 World Cup na jeet te.&quot; This heightened desire to win was evident in the team's approach to the 2024 T20 World Cup, where Sharma observed a distinct &quot;energy aur jo desperation&quot; among the players upon their arrival in the West Indies and the United States. He emphasized that &quot;failure aapko success se zyada cheezein sikhata hai.&quot;</p><p>This renewed determination bore fruit as India ended their 11-year wait for an ICC trophy by defeating South Africa in the 2024 T20 World Cup final in Barbados on June 29. The team continued its success, winning the 2025 Champions Trophy and another T20 World Cup title in 2026 under Suryakumar Yadav's captaincy. Reflecting on the emotional journey from the despair of Ahmedabad to the triumph in Barbados, Sharma delivered a poignant line: &quot;Har ek 19 November ke liye ek 29 June toh aata hi hai,&quot; encapsulating the stark contrast between the two significant dates in Indian cricket history.</p><p>What to watch: Future insights from Rohit Sharma on team building and leadership.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, correctly attributing quotes and context, and accurately captures the timeline and emotional narrative provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
