     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · BBC Sport · 2026-10-10 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Verstappen clinches Singapore pole ahead of Ferraris</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Formula 1</span> <span>#Singapore Grand Prix</span> <span>#Qualifying</span> <span>#Verstappen</span></div>
       <p>Red Bull driver Max Verstappen secured pole position for the Singapore Grand Prix on 10 October 2026, marking his second pole in a week after his sprint‑race win on Saturday. Verstappen's single flying lap of 1:31.373 was enough to stay ahead of Ferrari’s Charles Leclerc, who was 0.054 seconds slower, and Lewis Hamilton, who finished 0.123 seconds off the pace. The Dutch champion, fresh from his and Red Bull’s first season victory in Bahrain, described the car as “really strong all weekend” and said taking pole was “special”.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-10/verstappen-clinches-singapore-pole-ahead-of-ferraris.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Leclerc and Hamilton could not improve on their second runs, leaving the Ferraris in second and third. Mercedes rookie Kimi Antonelli posted the best Mercedes time, slotting fourth on the grid at +0.189 seconds, while team‑mate George Russell, despite being sixth fastest at +0.374 seconds, will start from the back after a penalty for excess engine parts. Antonelli’s result was highlighted as the first bright sign for the new Mercedes aerodynamic upgrade introduced in Malaysia, though the data suggest the package still lacks full accessibility.</p><p>McLaren’s Lando Norris qualified fifth (+0.339) and Oscar Piastri seventh (+0.487), the only driver outside the top‑four teams to improve on his second run. Alpine’s Pierre Gasly took eighth (+0.852), followed by Racing Bulls’ Liam Lawson (+0.996) and Arvid Lindblad (+1.408) to complete the top ten. The session followed a dramatic sprint race in which Verstappen claimed victory and Russell crashed out on the opening lap, underscoring the fine margins that defined qualifying.</p><p>Verstappen praised the weekend, saying “the car has been really strong all weekend” and that the team is on a “positive trend”. Leclerc admitted a “few small mistakes on his final lap” cost him pole, while Hamilton explained that an adjusted brake balance “tipped it a little over the edge” in Q3. The race is scheduled for Sunday, 11 October at 13:00 BST, with live commentary on BBC Radio 5 Sports Extra. What to watch: Verstappen’s race‑lead strategy against the Ferraris.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively incorporates the race results, specific qualifying times, driver quotes, and the context of the sprint race provided in the source.</p>
